WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 1, Programming foundations. You cannot debug a model if you cannot debug a script. This phase builds the working habits that make every later phase less mysterious: run Python, navigate a shell, version your work, and reproduce an environment. This phase has 3 lessons. Lesson 1: Set up a boring workspace. A clean environment, version control, clear folders, and repeatable commands turn an experiment into engineering work. The question for this lesson: Can someone else rerun your work?. You do not need a fancy setup. You need a boring one that works twice: once on your laptop today, and once on a fresh machine next month when the demo is due. Think of a project like a kitchen. The data is the ingredients, the code is the recipe, and the saved model is the dish. If the recipe only works while you are standing in the kitchen, it is not a recipe yet. You will learn: Use one virtual environment per project and pin its dependencies., Keep source, data, tests, and generated artifacts separate., Move durable logic out of notebooks and into scripts.. Practice: "Create a repository with a virtual environment. Watch out for: Global package installs that only work on your machine., Hiding all logic in one notebook., Committing secrets or raw private data.. You are ready when: Create a virtual environment., Pin dependencies., Document a quick start.. Lesson 2: Python you need for ML. Functions, data structures, imports, file I/O, and readable errors matter more than clever syntax. Write small scripts before writing models. The question for this lesson: Can you turn a notebook idea into a script that runs twice?. Most beginner ML code fails for ordinary Python reasons: a path is wrong, a function mutates a shared list, an exception is swallowed, or the script only works from one directory. Those are not model bugs. They are programming bugs. You do not need every language feature. You need functions with clear inputs, small modules, virtual environments, and errors that tell you what actually broke. That is enough to build the rest of the course. You will learn: Use functions to keep data loading, transforms, and training separate., Read tracebacks from the bottom up and fix the first real cause., Use pathlib, explicit encodings, and context managers for file work.. Practice: "Write a 60-line script that reads a CSV. Watch out for: Keeping all logic in notebook cells that must run in order., Catching every exception and printing a vague message., Using mutable defaults or global state without noticing.. You are ready when: Run the script from a clean shell., Trigger one useful error., Explain every function.. Recommended video: Learn Python — full course for beginners. Lesson 3: Git, Linux, and the command line. The shell is the control room. Learn paths, pipes, processes, file permissions, git status, diffs, branches, and safe commits. The question for this lesson: "Can you move around. A model job is mostly files, processes, and logs. The command line is how you see them. You do not need to memorize every flag; you need to know how to find the right command and verify what it did. Git is the undo button for ideas. Small commits with clear messages let you experiment, compare, and recover. A repository is also the first thing a reviewer opens, so keep it readable. You will learn: Absolute and relative paths explain most “file not found” errors., git status and git diff are safer than guessing what changed., Ignore data, environments, secrets, and generated artifacts early.. Practice: "Create a repo. Watch out for: Committing raw data, API keys, or virtual environments., Using destructive reset commands before checking status., Relying on the IDE to remember how the project is run.. You are ready when: Navigate with cd and ls., Read a diff., Commit only intended files.. A small project you can clone, run, inspect, and explain without a notebook open. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
